  1	//! # sealmap-model
  2	//!
  3	//! A language-neutral, **deterministic** model of a codebase: source files,
  4	//! the symbols they define, and the typed relations between those symbols.
  5	//!
  6	//! This crate is the shared vocabulary of the `sealmap` workspace. Language
  7	//! language adapters (such as `sealmap-rust`) *produce* a [`Codebase`]; projections
  8	//! (such as `sealmap-corpus`) *consume* one and render Mermaid diagrams from
  9	//! it. Because both sides only meet here, you can add a language adapter for another
 10	//! language, or a new diagram projection, without touching the other half.
 11	//!
 12	//! The navigation API is loosely inspired by
 13	//! [ts-morph](https://github.com/dsherret/ts-morph) (MIT, © David Sherret):
 14	//! a single [`Codebase`] value you can ask for files, symbols and relations,
 15	//! with an in-memory [`SourceSet`] standing in for the file system so tests and
 16	//! embedders never need to touch disk.
 17	//!
 18	//! ## Determinism contract
 19	//!
 20	//! Every type in this crate upholds the following, and downstream crates rely
 21	//! on it to produce byte-identical output across runs, machines and operating
 22	//! systems:
 23	//!
 24	//! 1. **Ordered collections only.** All maps and sets are `BTreeMap` /
 25	//!    `BTreeSet`; iteration order is a function of the data, never of a hash
 26	//!    seed or insertion order.
 27	//! 2. **Normalised paths.** [`SourcePath`] is always relative, `/`-separated
 28	//!    and free of `.`/`..` components, so `src\lib.rs` on Windows and
 29	//!    `src/lib.rs` on Linux are the same key.
 30	//! 3. **Stable hashing.** [`ContentHash`] is BLAKE3 over the source text with
 31	//!    line endings normalised to `\n`; it never uses `std`'s randomly seeded
 32	//!    hasher.
 33	//! 4. **No ambient data.** Nothing in the model records timestamps, absolute
 34	//!    paths, user names or environment variables.
 35	//!
 36	//! ## Built for sequence diagrams
 37	//!
 38	//! Callables carry a [`Flow`]: the ordered tree of calls, branches, loops and
 39	//! early exits in their body. That is what makes dense, per-function sequence
 40	//! diagrams possible without running the code, and because every call target
 41	//! is a canonical [`SymbolId`], separately rendered sequences can be merged or
 42	//! inlined into each other by a downstream orchestrator.
 43	//!
 44	//! ## Symbol ids
 45	//!
 46	//! Every symbol is named by a `sym:` id (see [`sym`]): kind-explicit,
 47	//! SCIP-style, with no file path, so moving code between files never changes
 48	//! an id. Beside the id, every symbol carries two [`Fingerprint`]s:
 49	//! `sig_hash` over its contract and `body_hash` over its implementation, so a
 50	//! tool can tell "unchanged", "behaviour changed" (body only) and "contract
 51	//! changed" (signature) apart, and can match a renamed symbol by its body.
 52	//!
 53	//! ## Example
 54	//!
 55	//! Build a tiny model by hand and query it:
 56	//!
 57	//! ```
 58	//! use sealmap_model::*;
 59	//!
 60	//! let pkg = Package::current("cargo", "demo").unwrap();
 61	//! let root = SymbolId::package_root(pkg.clone());
 62	//! let id = |name: &str| root.child(Descriptor::r#type(name)).unwrap();
 63	//!
 64	//! let mut cb = Codebase::new("demo");
 65	//! let file = SourcePath::new("src/lib.rs").unwrap();
 66	//! cb.add_file(SourceFile::new(file.clone(), "rust", root.clone(), "pub struct A;"));
 67	//!
 68	//! cb.add_symbol(Symbol::new(id("A"), "A", SymbolKind::Struct, file.clone()));
 69	//! cb.add_symbol(Symbol::new(id("B"), "B", SymbolKind::Struct, file.clone()));
 70	//! cb.add_relation(Relation::new(id("A"), id("B"), RelationKind::FieldType, Confidence::Exact));
 71	//!
 72	//! assert_eq!(id("A").to_string(), "sym:cargo demo . A#");
 73	//! assert_eq!(cb.symbols_in_file(&file).count(), 2);
 74	//! assert_eq!(cb.relations_from(&id("A")).count(), 1);
 75	//! ```
  1	//! `sealmap` command-line tool.
  2	//!
  3	//! ```text
  4	//! sealmap generate [PATH] -o docs/sealmap       # write / update the corpus
  5	//! sealmap verify   [PATH] -o docs/sealmap       # exit 1 on any drift (CI)
  6	//! sealmap model    [PATH]                        # print the model as JSON
  7	//! sealmap generate --repo api=../api --repo core=../core -o corpus
  8	//! ```
  9	
